<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Valencia</h1><p class="st-meta">1094 CE – 1102 CE · capital Valencia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Valencian principality of Rodrigo Díaz, El Cid, who conquered the city in 1094 and held it against the Almoravids until his death.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar</a>, a Castilian noble exiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>, had built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> career serving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-zaragoza">Taifa of Zaragoza</a> and collecting tribute in the east. When the qadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-jahhaf">Ibn Jahhaf</a> killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-al-qadir">al-Qadir</a> in 1092, Rodrigo besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, which surrendered in June 1094.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> ruled as an independent lord, nominally loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>. He defeated Almoravid relief armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cuarte">Cuarte</a> in 1094 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bairen">Bairén</a> in 1097, burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-jahhaf">Ibn Jahhaf</a> alive and turned the great mosque into a cathedral under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-perigord">Périgord</a>. Most of the Muslim population remained, paying taxes to his garrison.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> died in 1099. His widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimena-diaz">Jimena</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> until 1102, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> judged it indefensible, evacuated the Christians and burnt the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> entered in May 1102. The story inspired the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantar-de-mio-cid">Cantar de mio Cid</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-valencia.jpg" alt="Lordship of Valencia" width="320" height="331"><figcaption>Image: Wario2 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Se%C3%B1or%C3%ADo_de_Valencia_1099.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1094 CE – 1102 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Valencia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rodrigo Díaz (El Cid), Jimena Díaz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Castilian, Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Valencia, Valencia of El Cid</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1098&amp;focus=lordship-of-valencia" aria-label="Lordship of Valencia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4671 764 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Valencia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4991 1020L4991 1020M4991 1020L4799 1289"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4991 1020h0M4799 1289h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4991 1020h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4991 1020h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Valencia. <a href="/atlas?year=1098&amp;focus=lordship-of-valencia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
